/-
Split of Decomp445a.component2 into eight finite primality leaves.

The target asks: for each of the first 64 candidates `q` of the 10^90 row and
each prime divisor `p` of `q`, some `i < 16` satisfies
`p ^ 315 % M = q ^ (315 * i) % M`, for the fixed 5040-period modulus `M`
that this file writes out in full.

This file records the reduction the split rests on.  All 64 candidates are
prime.  A prime divisor of a prime candidate is that candidate itself, so the
witness is `i = 1` and the congruence is reflexivity.  The whole content of
the target is therefore the finite primality phase for those 64 integers:
no character, no p-adic input and no orbit computation is needed for this one
projection.

The finite primality phase is cut into eight blocks of eight displayed
integers, one leaf each, so a single leaf is a primality check on eight
written-out numbers instead of on sixty four.  The bridge from the fixed
candidate list `qs.take 64` to the eight displayed blocks is one finite list
identity, checked by the kernel and proved there.  The assembly
`component2_split` has no sorry of its own and does not use `component2`.

`component2_split` still inherits the sorry axiom from the eight leaves.  The
private theorem below is fully proved.
-/
